              "passageTitle": "The Long Journey of the Monarch Butterfly",
              "text": "Every autumn, millions of orange and black monarch butterflies leave Canada and the northern United States and fly south. Their journey can be as long as 4,000 kilometres, and it ends in a few mountain forests in central Mexico. There, the butterflies gather in such huge numbers that the branches of the trees bend under their weight.\n\nFor a long time, nobody knew where the monarchs went in winter. A Canadian scientist, Fred Urquhart, spent decades trying to answer this question. He and thousands of volunteers attached tiny paper labels to the wings of butterflies and asked anyone who found one to report it. Finally, in 1975, two members of his team found the winter forests in Mexico. Urquhart was so excited that he travelled there himself the following year.\n\nThe most surprising fact about this journey is that no single butterfly makes the whole round trip. Summer monarchs live for only two to six weeks. The generation that flies south, however, can live for up to eight months. In spring these butterflies start to fly north, lay their eggs and die. Their children and grandchildren complete the journey back to Canada. Somehow, each new generation knows the way, even though it has never made the trip before. Scientists believe the butterflies use the position of the sun and the Earth's magnetic field to find their direction.\n\nSadly, the number of monarchs has fallen sharply in recent decades. Their caterpillars can only eat one kind of plant, milkweed. Unfortunately, **it** is disappearing as farms grow larger and farmers use more chemicals. In Mexico, illegal cutting of trees has damaged the winter forests. Governments in all three countries are now working together, and many families plant milkweed in their gardens to help. As one Mexican guide says, 'The butterflies don't know borders. If we want to keep them, we have to protect them everywhere.'",
